# ══════════════════════════ CHAPTER 3 ══════════════════════════
{'num': 3, 'title': 'Workflow Configuration (Admin)',
 'blocks': [
  ('p', 'This chapter is for Super Admins and Tenant Admins. The Workflow Configuration console is the control room of the module: it defines how projects are approved, how stage gates route, when the system warns about over-allocation, which automation fires on approval, where timesheets route for sign-off, and what evidence closure requires. The console supports two scopes: <b>Tenant-wide (all companies)</b> and <b>company-scoped</b>. A company-scoped configuration always overrides the tenant-wide default for projects belonging to that company; where a company has no configuration, the tenant default applies, and where neither exists the platform defaults apply.'),
  ('nav', 'Sidebar / Workflow Configuration  (under Project Management)'),
  ('img', '11-workflow-config.png', 'Figure 3-1: The Workflow Configuration console - six governed sections with tenant-wide or company scope'),
  ('h2', '3.1  Section 1 - Project Initiation and Intake'),
  ('p', 'This section decides what a complete intake looks like. Toggles control whether the business objective, the executive sponsor and the target completion date are mandatory fields on the intake form; the delivery methodology is always mandatory. Below the toggles sits the <b>intake approval chain builder</b>. Each tier is a row with three parts: the approver source (for example Project Manager, Team Lead, Tenant Admin, or a named employee), a display label such as "Project Manager Review", and reorder plus delete controls. Tiers are executed strictly in order - tier 2 is only notified after tier 1 approves - and the project becomes active the moment the final tier approves.'),
  ('steps', [
    'Open Workflow Configuration and choose the scope (Tenant-wide or a specific company).',
    'In Section 1, switch on the fields your governance requires: business objective, executive sponsor, target completion date.',
    'In the Intake approval chain, set tier 1 (commonly Project Manager - "Project Manager Review") and tier 2 (commonly Tenant Admin - "Executive / Admin Approval").',
    'Add further tiers with + Add tier if your process needs finance or PMO review; use the arrow buttons to order them.',
    'Press Save. New submissions immediately follow the new chain; projects already in flight keep the chain they were submitted under.',
  ]),
  ('h2', '3.2  Section 2 - Stage Gate Review'),
  ('p', 'Stage gates route completed deliverables - and in Waterfall projects, phase completions - through a review chain before they count as accepted. Enable the toggle to activate gate reviews, then build the <b>gate approval chain</b> exactly like the intake chain. The recommended pattern is two tiers: a Project Manager or Team Lead tier labelled "Team Lead Review", followed by a Project Sponsor tier labelled "Project Sponsor Approval". The sponsor tier is what stamps the milestone with its approval status and actual date, so place the most senior accountable person last. Gates are per-project and per-deliverable: a gate decision applies to the submission at hand, not to the whole project.'),
  ('h2', '3.3  Section 3 - Capacity and Resource Rules'),
  ('p', 'Three controls govern resourcing safety. The <b>over-allocation threshold</b> (default 100 percent) determines when the capacity planner raises an alert: any employee whose combined confirmed plus soft allocations exceed the threshold in a week is flagged in red with the exact percentage and week. The <b>backup hand-off trigger</b> (default 5 working days) watches approved leave: when an allocated team member has an approved absence at least this long, the system notifies the PMs of every active project the person is allocated to, prompting a backup assignment. The <b>soft ("bench") reservations</b> toggle decides whether soft reservations count toward capacity while they wait for confirmation - keeping it on is recommended, because it is exactly what prevents two PMs from reserving the same bench person.'),
  ('callout', 'warning', 'Threshold discipline',
   'Raising the over-allocation threshold above 100 silences the warning but does not create capacity - the person still has a finite week. Keep 100 as the alert level and treat any red cell in the planner as a scheduling defect to fix, not noise to tune away.'),
  ('h2', '3.4  Section 4 - Automated Workspace'),
  ('p', 'On final intake approval the platform can assemble the project workspace automatically. The <b>auto-create collaboration chat room</b> toggle opens a dedicated project chat room; the <b>auto-create kanban delivery board</b> toggle creates a delivery board seeded from the project tasks, with every card two-way linked to its task record. Both surfaces live in the Collaboration module and are linked from the project workspace. Member notifications are always sent on approval. Disabling the toggles does not affect existing projects - automation runs once, at the moment of final approval.'),
  ('h2', '3.5  Section 5 - Timesheet Routing'),
  ('p', 'This section decides who approves billable time. The default routing chain is: the approver named on the employee\'s project allocation first; if none is set, the project\'s Project Manager; and as a last resort the submission is routed to the admin queue with a notification. The routing applies to every timesheet row submitted against the project. In Waterfall projects an additional hard rule applies regardless of routing: time cannot be logged against a task whose phase has not been approved yet - the submission is rejected with a clear phase-gate message naming the blocking phase.'),
  ('h2', '3.6  Section 6 - Closure Checklist and Sprint Defaults'),
  ('p', 'Closure is evidence-driven. The checklist section defines which items the readiness report demands before the sign-off button becomes available - typical items are all stage gates approved, milestones stamped, timesheets fully approved and the post-mortem completed per allocated member. Sprint defaults set the organisation\'s standard sprint length in weeks, which pre-fills new sprints on Agile projects. As everywhere in the console, Save applies the configuration to the selected scope only, and Reset restores the platform defaults for that scope.'),
  ('callout', 'tip', 'Pilot per company',
   'Introduce stricter chains by scoping them to a pilot company first. Company scope overrides tenant defaults, so you can trial a three-tier intake for one subsidiary and promote it tenant-wide once the process has proven itself.'),
 ]},

